        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Interpretation\n",
        "\n",
        "### Do liked items really affect AUC/loss that much?\n",
        "\n",
        "On the `2026-11-01` holdout (n_train=165, n_test=50):\n",
        "\n",
        "| Metric | liked (current) | perfect only | Delta |\n",
        "|--------|----------------|--------------|-------|\n",
        "| Fitted AUC | ~0.698 | ~0.748 | **+0.050** |\n",
        "| Fitted logLoss | ~0.380 | ~0.371 | **−0.009** |\n",
        "\n",
        "**Yes — but in the opposite direction one might expect.** Excluding liked items from clustering *improved* fitted AUC by about **0.05** (≈7% relative) and slightly reduced logLoss. The keyword baseline (`rel`) is unchanged between conditions (≈0.88), confirming the difference comes from the LR model's features — specifically the `n:d1` distance-to-medoid feature — not from the data.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Why might liked items hurt?\n",
        "\n",
        "Liked items are recent and personally salient, so they pull medoids toward the user's *recent* tastes. But the holdout test set is chronological (future dates), so medoids anchored to recent likes may not generalize forward. Perfect samples are durable, representative anchors that generalize better across time. The ablation suggests liked items add noise to the medoid positions, slightly degrading out-of-sample discrimination.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Caveats\n",
        "\n",
        "* Test set is small (n_test ≈ 50 with test_frac=0.2), so a ΔAUC of 0.05 may not be statistically significant on a single date.\n",
        "* Results vary by date and embedding model (see `model_eval` table for historical fitted_auc values per embedding tag).\n",
        "* The prior AUC also shifts between conditions because the prior uses the `n:d1` feature whose values depend on the medoids.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Reproducing\n",
        "\n",
        "```bash\n",
        "# Run the Sacred experiment (persists to docs/experiments/runs.db):\n",
        "python -m simulate.cli clustering-ablation --date 2026-11-01 --test-frac 0.2\n",
        "\n",
        "# Or re-run on a different date:\n",
        "python -m simulate.cli clustering-ablation --date 2026-10-29 --test-frac 0.2\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The Node.js helper is `ranker/eval_clustering_ablation.js`; the Sacred experiment is `simulate/experiments/clustering_ablation.py` (`daily_brief_clustering_ablation`)."
      ],
